% =====================================================================
% ABSTRACT
% =====================================================================

\begin{abstract}
Sustainability reporting is now mandatory for India's largest listed companies, producing a large volume of long annual reports that cannot be reviewed manually. Existing automated methods mostly analyze language style to detect greenwashing -- but a claim can sound convincing even when the reported numbers do not support it. This paper presents GRASP, a pipeline that checks what a company says against what the same company has actually reported as a measured figure. The system classifies each disclosure under the relevant BRSR principle, identifies vague or unsupported statements, checks improvement claims against filed numerical values, measures alignment with regulatory wording, and produces a Transparency Score comparable across industry peers. The classifier achieves macro-F1 of 0.658, the greenwashing detector achieves F1~=~0.880 and AUC-ROC~=~0.894, and the Transparency Score correlates with independent human ratings at $r = 0.631$ ($p = 0.003$). GRASP provides a practical way to reduce a large national reporting corpus to the disclosures and companies that deserve closer review.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Sustainability reporting, greenwashing detection, ESG disclosure, transparency scoring, weak supervision, domain-adaptive pretraining, BERT.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% =====================================================================
% SECTION I -- INTRODUCTION
% =====================================================================

\section{Introduction}

India's largest listed companies are now required to publish annual sustainability reports using a standardized format called the Business Responsibility and Sustainability Reporting (BRSR) framework. This format covers nine principles ranging from environmental responsibility and employee welfare to governance and consumer rights \cite{sebi2021,ngrbc2019}. Sustainability disclosure has therefore shifted from a voluntary public-relations activity to a formal regulatory requirement.

This shift has created a practical problem. Hundreds of companies submit reports every year, many exceeding 100 pages. Regulators and investors cannot review all of this content in detail. The volume of information has grown faster than the ability to verify it.

This gap creates room for greenwashing. A company may describe its environmental performance in positive terms without providing any measured evidence. For example, a company may state that it is committed to reducing emissions or making steady progress on water conservation, without reporting a single number to support either claim. Such statements are hard to disprove because they do not make a specific, falsifiable assertion. Research on Indian BRSR filings confirms this pattern: vague language and missing evidence are more common than outright false claims \cite{shankar2024,garg2025}.

Three important gaps exist in current automated approaches. \textbf{First}, most methods judge a disclosure only by its language -- they check whether it sounds specific, hedged, or confident. No existing work directly compares what a company claims with the numerical values the same company has filed. \textbf{Second}, most studies evaluate individual components in isolation, not a complete end-to-end system that starts from a national filing corpus and produces a ranked, company-level output. \textbf{Third}, methods that depend on expert annotations or external performance databases are difficult to scale across hundreds of companies without significant manual effort.

GRASP is designed to address all three gaps. It collects and parses BRSR filings automatically, classifies each disclosure under the relevant regulatory principle without manual annotation, detects vague or contradictory claims by comparing narrative text against filed numerical values, measures how well each disclosure aligns with its governing principle, and produces a single Transparency Score for every company. The score is benchmarked against companies in the same industry sector, so a company's standing is always relative to its peers. The remainder of this paper reviews related work, describes the seven-stage pipeline in detail, reports empirical results for each stage, and concludes with directions for future development.

