% SECTION II -- LITERATURE REVIEW
% =====================================================================

\section{Literature Review}

Related work falls into two broad areas: (1) automated greenwashing detection from sustainability text, and (2) studies of disclosure quality in Indian BRSR filings. A recent survey of greenwashing detection from corporate text \cite{calamai2025} gives a broader overview of the methods used in this area; the studies below are the ones most relevant to the problem GRASP addresses.

\subsection{Automated Greenwashing Detection}

Florstedt \textit{et al.}\ \cite{florstedt2025} combined sentiment, linguistic specificity, and claim consistency to build a Perceived-Real Gap Score; applied to 150 European reports, the method flagged 43.5\% as showing greenwashing characteristics, though it requires external performance data per company. Vinella \textit{et al.}\ \cite{vinella2024} compared language-model architectures for greenwashing detection and found that performance depended more on architectural choices than on model scale, though the evaluation covered individual claims rather than complete filings. Zhang \cite{zhang2025} studied the distinction between hedged language and direct, quantified commitments, providing a useful vocabulary for identifying weak sustainability statements; however, the approach cannot determine whether a strong claim is actually backed by reported data. Ong \textit{et al.}\ \cite{ong2025} tested ESG analysis methods on categories unseen during training and found that domain adaptation improved robustness more reliably than scaling up model size, without constructing an end-to-end system.

\subsection{Indian BRSR Disclosure Quality}

Shankar and Xu \cite{shankar2024} built a sentence-level classifier for Indian sustainability reports and found that vagueness and missing evidence are more common than measurable false claims, though the model depends on expert-annotated data which limits scalability. Garg \textit{et al.}\ \cite{garg2025} analyzed 1,012 BRSR filings and showed that reporting quality varies widely and that unquantified narrative is common, but no detection pipeline was built. Sisodia \cite{sisodia2024} compared fine-tuned transformers with large language models for Indian annual reports and found that smaller fine-tuned models achieve comparable accuracy at lower cost. Raman \textit{et al.}\ \cite{raman2023} mapped local sustainability frameworks to United Nations Sustainable Development Goals, showing that local frameworks can be connected to global references.

\noindent Together, these studies confirm that hedged language is a useful signal, domain adaptation helps, and Indian filings contain significant unquantified content. However, none of the above work directly compares narrative claims against a company's own filed numerical values, none produces a complete national-scale end-to-end pipeline, and none generates a sector-comparable score without external data or expert annotation. GRASP is designed to address all three gaps.

