\section{Proposed Solution}

GRASP is built around one central idea: instead of judging a sustainability claim only by how it is written, the system also checks whether the claim is supported by the quantitative values the same company has filed. This directly addresses all three gaps identified in Section~II.

The pipeline has seven stages, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture}. Stages~1--3 acquire and parse the filings, derive classification labels from the regulatory structure without expert annotation, and train a domain-adapted nine-class principle classifier. Stage~4 applies three parallel detection signals -- a hedging lexicon check, a semantic similarity check, and a claim-versus-filed-figure check -- and combines them into a single greenwashing flag. Stage~5 measures how closely each disclosure matches the wording of its governing principle. Stage~6 combines four measures into a Transparency Score and benchmarks each company against peers in the same industry sector. Stage~7 exposes the results through a service layer. Each stage is described in Section~IV, and its results are reported in Section~V. Both the narrative claims and the numerical values used for verification come from the same regulatory filing, so no external dataset is needed for the main verification step.

