\section{Methodology}

\subsection{Stage 1: Filing Acquisition and Parsing}

BRSR filings are retrieved from the national source index through an automated download script that processes companies sequentially and maintains a progress log, marking each entry as completed or failed. Interrupted runs resume from the last checkpoint without reprocessing completed entries. Each filing is parsed exactly once; every numerical value is stored as a \emph{fact} with three attributes: the metric it measures, the reporting year, and the applicable sub-group if one exists (for example, gender category or employee type). Values are stored exactly as filed without conversion. The data model has three linked entities --- company, filing, and fact --- and a uniqueness constraint prevents duplicate ingestion of the same filing.

\subsection{Stage 2: Label Derivation and Boilerplate Removal}

Training labels are derived directly from the BRSR format: every text segment in a filing is tagged with the principle (one of nine) that governs it, so no manual annotation is required. This label derivation from the regulatory structure is what enables the method to scale. A practical complication arises because many filings copy the same general policy statement under several principles; the same paragraph may then carry multiple conflicting labels, which degrades classifier training. To resolve this, segments are grouped by company, concept, and exact text, and any segment appearing under more than one principle for the same company is removed from the training data. The cleaned data is split into training, validation, and test sets (70/15/15) using stratified sampling, so each principle is represented in all three sets in the same proportion.

\subsection{Stage 3: Domain Adaptation and Principle Classifier}

The classifier is trained in two steps. First, the \texttt{bert-base-uncased} model \cite{devlin2019} is further trained with masked language modelling on unlabelled BRSR text (corpus size reported in Section~V-C). This domain-adaptive pre-training step requires no labels and teaches the model the vocabulary and style of sustainability reports before the supervised task begins; prior work confirms this approach improves results on specialised text \cite{ong2025,gururangan2020}. Second, a single linear layer that maps the 768-dimensional encoder output to the nine BRSR principles is appended and fine-tuned using PyTorch \cite{paszke2019} and the Hugging Face Transformers library \cite{wolf2020}, with a maximum sequence length of 512, four training epochs, learning rate $2 \times 10^{-5}$, the AdamW optimizer \cite{loshchilov2019}, cross-entropy loss, and linear warmup over the first 10\% of steps.

\subsection{Stage 4: Greenwashing Detection}

Three checks are applied to every disclosure in parallel, and their results are then combined.

\subsubsection{Hedging Lexicon Check}

Hedging phrases are soft expressions that promise something without providing measurable proof. A curated lexicon of approximately thirty such phrases covers four categories: aspirational intent (e.g., ``aims to'', ``strives to''), conditional commitments (e.g., ``where possible'', ``subject to''), vague temporal references (e.g., ``in the near future''), and unverifiable quantities (e.g., ``significant progress''). A complementary evidence-pattern set identifies specificity markers: percentages, currency values, physical units, named certifications, and specific financial years. A segment is flagged when it contains at least one hedging phrase and no evidence pattern.

\subsubsection{Semantic Similarity Check}

Each sentence is encoded as a dense embedding capturing its meaning \cite{reimers2019,song2020}, then compared against two ten-sentence reference sets: one composed of vague statements and one of quantified statements. A segment is evaluated by its most specific sentence, so a single generic opening line cannot cause an otherwise substantive paragraph to be flagged. A segment is treated as vague when its most specific sentence is less than 70\% similar to the quantified reference set. This threshold was selected by optimising precision-recall balance on the labelled set; a higher threshold increases recall at the cost of precision.

\subsubsection{Claim-Versus-Filed-Figure Verification}

This check compares the narrative text against the numerical values the same company has filed. A fixed set of environmental and social metrics is tracked (for example, total emissions, water consumption, and waste generated). For each tracked metric, the previous and current filed values are retrieved from the fact database to determine whether the value increased or decreased. Sentences that express improvement and reference the same metric are then identified; if a sentence claims improvement but the filed value increased, a contradiction is recorded. Evaluation is performed sentence by sentence, ensuring that a claim about one metric is never matched against the trend of a different one. When available, each contradiction is additionally tagged with the third-party assurance level of the corresponding number. Algorithm~\ref{alg:contradiction} shows the full procedure.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Claim-Versus-Filed-Figure Verification}
\label{alg:contradiction}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Filing $F$, tracked concepts $C$, narrative segments $N$
\Ensure Set of contradiction signals $S$
\State $S \gets \emptyset$
\State $N \gets \text{DEDUPLICATE}(N)$
\ForAll{$c \in C$}
    \State $(v_{\text{prev}}, v_{\text{curr}}) \gets \text{TREND}(F, c)$
    \If{$v_{\text{prev}}$ is None \textbf{or} $v_{\text{curr}} \leq v_{\text{prev}}$}
        \State \textbf{continue} \Comment{no rise}
    \EndIf
    \ForAll{$n \in N$}
        \ForAll{sentence $t \in \text{SPLIT}(n)$}
            \If{$\text{IMPROVES}(t)$ \textbf{and} $\text{REFERS}(t, c)$}
                \State $a \gets \text{ASSURANCE}(F, c)$
                \State $\sigma \gets \text{SEVERITY}(a)$
                \State $S \gets S \cup \{(c, t, v_{\text{prev}}, v_{\text{curr}}, a, \sigma)\}$
            \EndIf
        \EndFor
    \EndFor
\EndFor
\State \Return $S$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\textbf{Putting the three checks together.} A segment is flagged as vague if the hedging check or the similarity check fires. The claim-versus-figure check adds its own contradiction record for each sentence it catches. The system records which check fired, enabling independent audit of each signal.

\subsection{Stage 5: Regulatory Alignment Scoring}

The alignment score measures how closely a disclosure matches the official wording of its governing BRSR principle. The exact text of each of the nine principles is encoded into an embedding once at startup; each cleaned text segment is then compared against all nine embeddings using cosine similarity, and a company's alignment score for a principle is the average similarity across its segments. The score is validated with a top-1 accuracy test: a segment is counted as correctly aligned when the intended principle ranks highest among all nine. With nine principles, random guessing yields 11.1\%. The alignment score does not measure regulatory compliance; it measures topical relevance.

\subsection{Stage 6: Transparency Score and Sector Benchmark}

The Transparency Score ($TS$) combines four parts:

\begin{equation}
TS = 0.40\,C + 0.30\,Sp + 0.20\,Cl + 0.10\,SDG
\label{eq:ts}
\end{equation}

\noindent where:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Completeness ($C$)} is the share of tracked numerical items for which the company reports a real (non-trivial) value.
\item \textbf{Specificity ($Sp$)} is the share of text segments that contain at least one piece of quantified evidence.
\item \textbf{Clarity ($Cl$)} is one minus the share of segments flagged as vague.
\item \textbf{SDG Alignment ($SDG$)} is the share of United Nations Sustainable Development Goals that are linked to evidence-backed disclosures \cite{raman2023}. A goal counts only if it is linked to a disclosure with supporting evidence, not if it is merely mentioned.
\end{itemize}

Completeness has the highest weight because filing real measured values is the most direct sign of good disclosure.

Each company's score is then expressed as a percentile within its industry sector. The sector is determined by the first two digits of the national industry classification code. Sectors with fewer than five companies are treated as statistically unreliable.

\subsection{Stage 7: Service Layer}

The service layer exposes every stage through typed API endpoints. Each endpoint calls an analysis function that has already been validated. Available operations include listing companies, returning a full analysis report, showing the Transparency Score breakdown, displaying audit flags, retrieving sector rankings, uploading new filings, and comparing groups of companies. The upload endpoint uses the same ingestion process as the bulk download script, ensuring all filings are treated identically.

\section{Implementation and Results}

This section reports the results of each stage in sequence. Stage methodology is described in Section~IV. The output of each stage feeds the next.

\subsection{Corpus Coverage (Stage 1)}

The download script processed 1,276 companies from the national source index; of these, 1,164 were successfully loaded into the fact database. The remaining companies could not be processed because their downloaded files were corrupted or incomplete, as confirmed by file-integrity checks. All 1,164 companies are used in all subsequent stages.

\subsection{Training Data (Stage 2)}

After removing repeated text segments, \textbf{4,854 labelled segments} remain across the nine BRSR principles and are split into training, validation, and test sets. Prior to deduplication, most candidate segments carried conflicting labels; removing them yields reliable training data without any manual labelling.

\subsection{Principle Classifier (Stage 3)}

\subsubsection{Domain Adaptation}

