\subsubsection{Rule-Based vs.\ Learned Detector}

An XGBoost classifier \cite{chen2016} was trained on six features and evaluated with leave-one-out cross-validation on 80 labelled segments: hedging-term count, hedging density, word count, similarity to the vague reference set, similarity to the quantified reference set, and presence of a KPI-related phrase. The rule-based detector achieves higher F1 (0.732 vs.\ 0.675); XGBoost achieves higher AUC-ROC (0.764 vs.\ 0.731), indicating better probabilistic ranking but less accurate binary decisions at this dataset size. The two strongest features are similarity to the vague set (0.366) and KPI presence (0.262); raw hedging count contributes nothing (0.000), supporting the design choice of the rule-based detector. Results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:rule_vs_xgb}.

