% CONCLUSION
% =========================================================

\section{Conclusion}

India's mandatory sustainability reporting has produced a large volume of disclosure content that cannot be reviewed manually at scale. Most existing automated methods evaluate how convincing the language of a disclosure sounds -- useful but insufficient, since a well-written claim may still be unsupported by any reported data. GRASP addresses this by checking what a company says against what the same company has actually filed. Each of the three gaps identified in Section~II is directly resolved: GRASP compares narrative claims with filed numerical values, delivers a national-scale end-to-end pipeline from filing download to ranked output, and derives training labels from the regulatory structure without expert annotation. Empirical evaluation across 1,164 companies demonstrates the pipeline's operational effectiveness: the classifier achieves macro-F1~=~0.658, the greenwashing detector achieves F1~=~0.880 and AUC-ROC~=~0.894, and the Transparency Score correlates with independent human ratings at $r = 0.631$. Beyond cross-sectional benchmarking, longitudinal evaluation confirms that the score captures real inter-temporal progress, revealing an average +5.23-point progression across consecutive filing cycles driven predominantly by quantitative completeness gains.

This work can be extended across multiple levels. At the data level, ingestion capabilities can be expanded to extract unstructured annexures and multi-modal charts that supplement corporate filings. At the modeling level, automated text analysis of company operational descriptions can refine sector classifications beyond static registration codes (addressing known boundary cases such as Meghmani Organics or Poly Medicure). At the longitudinal level, as annual reporting cycles accumulate across India's top 1,000 listed firms, multi-year tracking can be scaled into a full market-wide panel to assess long-term disclosure trends. Finally, at the policy level, the core cross-verification approach can be adapted to global sustainability standards, such as the EU Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) and ISSB frameworks, where structured disclosures provide a comparable baseline for automated evaluation.

